00:01:15,840 --> 00:01:20,708
In an ideal world, we would only have two browsers, A and B.

21
00:01:21,294 --> 00:01:28,836
They would connect directly, no plugins, no add-ins, no extra hops, zero latency.

22
00:01:29,134 --> 00:01:32,612
The problem becomes we don't have any public addresses anymore.

23
00:01:33,006 --> 00:01:44,260
Now you might have a lot of smart devices inside your network, and you might also feel like because they're communicating with the internet, they would have a public IP address.

24
00:01:44,654 --> 00:01:49,444
That is not true because they all get assigned a private IP address.

25
00:01:50,382 --> 00:02:05,600
And when and only when they try and communicate to the outer internet, our router, which is provided by the ISB, assigns a publicly reachable IP address to that device.

26
00:02:05,840 --> 00:02:12,720
The problem becomes when the remote connection tries to reach out, the inbound might be blocked.

27
00:02:12,974 --> 00:02:17,540
Now it could be the router, it could also be the firewall which will talk ahead.

28
00:02:17,966 --> 00:02:26,720
Now every network or at least the secure networks are equipped with a firewall which determines which protocols and ports are allowed.

29
00:02:26,880 --> 00:02:37,920
Now, in simple terms, a protocol is a set of rules that determines how the bytes and data must be structured so that two systems can communicate with each other.

30
00:02:38,240 --> 00:02:46,852
Port is the number that gets assigned to an endpoint that determines which network traffic is meant for that endpoint.

31
00:02:47,886 --> 00:02:54,160
Now, most of the UDP ports, which is use the Datagram protocol, they're all blocked.

32
00:02:54,414 --> 00:03:02,884
And UDP ports 3478, which are mostly used by stun and turn servers, are often blocked, depends on how restrictive the network might be.

33
00:03:03,406 --> 00:03:08,720
One port that is rarely blocked is the web traffic port, which is port 443.

34
00:03:09,102 --> 00:03:15,680
So we just bound to mask our traffic as 443 in case the first two options fail.

35
00:03:15,900 --> 00:03:22,980
All right, let's suppose we've been able to successfully mask our traffic and it can bypass the firewall.

36
00:03:23,406 --> 00:03:30,500
We run into our next problem is the devices would not know what their own public IP address is.

37
00:03:30,990 --> 00:03:37,280
That's where stun comes into picture, which is Session Transversal Utilities for NAT.

38
00:03:37,678 --> 00:03:39,600
Now there's several stun servers.

39
00:03:39,680 --> 00:03:44,240
There's one for Google that mostly gets used, which is publicly accessible.

40
00:03:44,400 --> 00:03:53,924
What it does basically is your device will reach out to the stun server with one specific message, what is my public IP address?

41
00:03:54,160 --> 00:04:04,320
And based on the incoming address that the stun server sees, it responds back by telling our device, hey, you look like this public IP address.

42
00:04:04,480 --> 00:04:07,460
There are cases where stun is not enough.

43
00:04:07,982 --> 00:04:11,360
There's something we call symmetric NAT.

44
00:04:12,590 --> 00:04:20,000
What symmetric NAT does is for every endpoint that you try to reach, it will assign them a different port number.

45
00:04:20,160 --> 00:04:32,560
So when we reach the or our device rather reaches the stun server, it responds by saying, Hey, you seem to be this specific IP address with a port number 52001.

46
00:04:33,070 --> 00:04:40,964
Now you take your IP address with a port number, you send that through to your friend you're trying to make the video call with.

47
00:04:42,094 --> 00:04:51,620
And when your friend tries to reach out, because your friend is not a stun server, there'll be a different port number that gets opened for that connection.

48
00:04:51,854 --> 00:04:59,492
Now, to solve that problem, we get to turn, which is transversal using relay around NAT.

49
00:05:00,014 --> 00:05:06,064
It's a fancy acronym to say, hey, I can't get to you, let's meet in the middle.

50
00:05:06,190 --> 00:05:12,868
So what happens is all the media that we have gets relayed from a middleman, if you may.

51
00:05:13,646 --> 00:05:21,540
So all the data from me and my friend gets sent to this cloud relay, which carries out the communication between us two.

52
00:05:22,510 --> 00:05:28,784
There is a trade-off that it is carrying all of the media, so there might be some security concerns around it.

53
00:05:28,974 --> 00:05:34,864
Not to mention it adds an additional hop, causing latency and higher costs as well.

54
00:05:35,104 --> 00:05:35,824
Now, perfect.

55
00:05:35,984 --> 00:05:38,948
So this resolves how we can communicate.

56
00:05:39,664 --> 00:05:47,268
What decides, like based on circumstances between me and my friend and our setup?

57
00:05:48,814 --> 00:05:55,344
Who decides which connections can be made, which one is successful, which one's most viable?

58
00:05:55,504 --> 00:05:57,860
That's where ice come into play.

59
00:05:58,304 --> 00:06:10,944
Interactive connectivity establishment is the orchestrator which decides how we're gonna connect or which path are we gonna take if there's three different ways to get to the destination.

60
00:06:11,184 --> 00:06:13,264
Ice is that framework.

61
00:06:13,614 --> 00:06:20,580
Framework is what uses a tool call or different protocols to carry out a certain functionality.

62
00:06:21,582 --> 00:06:27,876
So in this case, ICE is a framework that uses both stun and turn protocols together.

63
00:06:28,144 --> 00:06:46,308
It basically calculates the cost of each of the connection and the best possible connection if me and the person I'm trying to connect to, if we exist on the same network, then we can connect directly without any hop, latency, or additional cost.

64
00:06:47,424 --> 00:06:58,544
And if the connection is being successful by using the returned IP address and port number through the stun server, we should be able to do it.

65
00:06:58,704 --> 00:07:06,384
But in cases where there's symmetric NAT at play, we will have to use turn server, which will add additional cost.

66
00:07:06,766 --> 00:07:09,744
So chronologically, it'll go from one, two, three.

67
00:07:09,966 --> 00:07:15,172
Whichever connection it sees viable first is the connection is gonna go ahead with.

68
00:07:15,726 --> 00:07:17,664
Now all this works perfectly.

69
00:07:17,904 --> 00:07:25,264
The most important question is how do two devices even locate each other on the public internet?

70
00:07:25,424 --> 00:07:27,104
There's thousands of devices.

71
00:07:27,470 --> 00:07:33,188
How do you know you're connecting with your friends mobile and friends mobile only, not a stranger?

72
00:07:33,614 --> 00:07:35,984
The answer is a signaling server.

73
00:07:36,144 --> 00:07:38,436
In simple terms, is a matchmaker.

74
00:07:38,766 --> 00:07:46,148
It allows you to find your friend's device on this public internet that's filled with billions and billions of devices.

75
00:07:46,544 --> 00:07:55,584
It does that by using something we call SDP, which is a session description protocol.

76
00:07:56,366 --> 00:08:03,172
It takes your SDP offer from your phone and sends it to your friend's phone.

77
00:08:03,502 --> 00:08:09,984
It goes through all the specifications and responses with an SDP answer.

78
00:08:10,222 --> 00:08:14,020
Now let's understand what SDP actually encapsulates.

79
00:08:14,318 --> 00:08:19,524
Right at the top we have the version number for SDP, which is most of the cases zero.

80
00:08:19,822 --> 00:08:30,084
Then we have different attributes which start off with the user ID, which is blank in this case, followed by a session ID and a session version number.

81
00:08:31,182 --> 00:08:44,580
This is for internet, type of address IPv4, and a placeholder for IP address, because we haven't fully authenticated to each device to figure out their actual IP address that can be included.

82
00:08:45,486 --> 00:08:48,132
S is the session ID, in this case is blank.

83
00:08:49,198 --> 00:08:53,056
T is here for the timestamp, so start and stop.

84
00:08:53,216 --> 00:09:02,576
We can see both start and stop are zero, which basically means no timestamps or session duration were mentioned in this specific message.

85
00:09:02,896 --> 00:09:08,036
M is for, as you can see, a media line, so it opens a media channel.

86
00:09:08,526 --> 00:09:11,332
The first line is for audio, then a video.

87
00:09:12,558 --> 00:09:16,176
This here is a placeholder for the port number.

88
00:09:16,256 --> 00:09:21,536
It carries no meaning until we receive the ICE candidates, then it gets mapped to it.

89
00:09:21,696 --> 00:09:24,100
The protocol being followed, which is UDP.

90
00:09:24,942 --> 00:09:26,320
Transport their security.

91
00:09:26,320 --> 00:09:33,300
It uses DTLS, which is basically TLS for insecure protocols like UDP.

92
00:09:33,536 --> 00:09:47,216
RTP is for real-time transport protocol, which is for audio video packets, audio video, profile feedback, basically a feedback loop on the RTP protocol, so the packets that you're sending.

93
00:09:47,456 --> 00:09:58,096
Our payload ID, which gets mapped to something we receive from a prefix of A, which is attribute, which is RTP map.

94
00:09:58,256 --> 00:10:06,176
So speaking of attribute, which is RTP map, it basically maps a payload to a coda decoder.

95
00:10:06,446 --> 00:10:11,844
As we saw, 111 and 96 were the payloads in the media lines.

96
00:10:12,302 --> 00:10:21,476
Now we're binding 111 to Opus, which is for audio, and 96 to VP8, which is for video.

97
00:10:22,126 --> 00:10:26,148
This number here is the RTP timestamp.

98
00:10:26,670 --> 00:10:33,216
So the receiver knows how to receive them and order them correctly with correct time spacing.

99
00:10:33,376 --> 00:10:36,036
So next up we have the ice credentials.

100
00:10:36,366 --> 00:10:41,856
So these are the credentials that we use later to authenticate between phone A and phone B.

101
00:10:42,016 --> 00:10:46,096
This is the username, and that is the password we're going to use to authenticate.

102
00:10:46,360 --> 00:10:48,496
A Couple of other attributes worth mentioning.

103
00:10:48,656 --> 00:10:58,576
A hash of the certificate, which is used during DTLS handcheck, so we know this is the correct entity that we negotiated with initially.

104
00:10:58,736 --> 00:11:05,956
The second one is A equals send receive, which signifies this is for a two-way call.

105
00:11:06,478 --> 00:11:10,500
If this would have been send only, that means you're broadcasting.

106
00:11:10,766 --> 00:11:15,136
If it would have been receive only, that means you only a viewer or a listener.

107
00:11:15,598 --> 00:11:21,376
If it would have been inactive, it means none of the communication was happening through this specific channel.

108
00:11:21,550 --> 00:11:36,816
Alright, perfect, and then SDP message is being sent to the phone B, which creates its answers on what all technologies it can support and why the signaling server that message is then transported back to the phone A.

